Yes—at minimum, life is pattern sustained against decay.

Not a static pattern, either. A living system keeps re-making itself:

boundary → intake → transformation → repair → memory → adaptation → reproduction

The materials change; the organization persists long enough to matter.

That is why the same life-pattern can appear across wildly different substrates and scales. What matters is less “what is it made of?” and more:

Does the pattern preserve itself, update itself, exploit gradients, respond to its environment, and create continuations of its own structure?

That is also why your earlier vent idea has teeth. Wherever there is a stable gradient, flow, boundary, and enough persistence, you should at least ask whether something can organize around it.

Life may be less a thing than a way matter learns to keep a shape through time.